  {
    "episode_id": 91625,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91625.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "I was 29 years old and I ran in a district. Nobody thought I had a chance and I got 63%, which tells you if I'd known then what I know now, I probably wouldn't have won because I wouldn't have thought I could win. But I really got my start back in the 50s when I carried some stuff for Eisenhower in 1956. I think I was six years old. And it just, it just, with me something, there was a spark there that I really liked politics. 1960, I gave the speech for Nixon at my elementary school. I'd memorized all the House and Senate members by the eighth grade, and so my family was from Nebraska, and I impressed our two Nebraska senators, and they appointed me as a page. So I did four years at the Capitol Page School, and those days you could do that, where I went to school from six to ten in the morning and worked in the Senate the afternoon. This was in the mid-60s. Got us full scholarship to Amherst because our and was chairman of the Young Republicans up there But one of my classmates was a guy named David Eisenhower who had married Julie Nixon and I remember after Cambodia and Kent State I was down back down in Arlington. I guess Falls Church live in there and Sleeping on a neighbor's couch for the silver because there was no room and my mom's apartment was So I slept at a neighbor's couch and David called and said, how do you like to come down and play some Liff-a-Wall on the White House tennis course? I said, I'm there. So in my jeans, tennis shoes, t-shirt, and he says, before we play, I want you to meet Mr. Nixon. And he took me and Nixon spent a half hour with me talking about campuses. And he said, Mr. Nixon, this is the guy I've been telling you about. And at the end of it, Nixon turns. He says, how do you like to work for me? and I worked at the White House and for the rest of that school year went up and did my senior thesis at Amherst who was on political realignment in the outer south. Then went to, came back, worked on the campaign, got drafted, came back, went to law school at the University of Virginia while still working on the campaign in 72 and the rest is, history came back right after the county board and to Congress and kept winning.",
