    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "had to knock off the incumbent campaign chairman who was Gingrich's guy. I beat him solidly in the caucus because in 1998 we lost seats and the members had expected us to win seats. So I saw an opening. If it was a factuated race, I wasn't sure where I'd stand, but I got out early in front with about 30 or 40 public supporters and scared everybody else off. So I got John Linder, who was the chairman, one-on-one. And at that point, it was mine. Now, I had the gun groups, everybody opposing me, because my gun record, as you can imagine, in an urban district with high rises is going to be a little bit different than people living out. It wasn't liberal, but it was not I think I had a D from the NRA. But I brought the NRA to Fairfax. They were headquartered there when I was the head of the county government. I brought them there. So what it would mean is I'm just recognized the constituency I represented and trying to represent their views. So I ran for campaign chairman. We had a five-seat lead in the house. If you go back to the 2000 election, every pundit in this town predicted we'd lose the house, and we lost the Senate. We lost the presidency until it went to the Supreme Court. But we just, we outsmarted and we ran better races, and then they would ask me to do it again in 2002. And I didn't want to do it in 2002. We held the presidency. You always lose seats when you're, we picked up eight seats. So I had a good record and I used that to become a chairmanship of a committee and jumped eight people in seniority.",
