  {
    "episode_id": 91625,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91625.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 22,
    "turn_text": "Well, you got a lot of digital stuff now. I'm not sure I understand all that. My first race, I simply bought the voting list from the state. I bought an alphabetical list and I bought a spreadsheet. And Xerox the street sheet, and I just took that around and started ringing doors. And I rang everybody's doors who was a registered voter. And I looked up and knew if they'd voted in a primary before in what their inclination is, but there's no party registration. And then they all got a note from me within two weeks saying it was nice meeting you while I made a personal note about it. And then the week before the election, they got another note saying it was nice meeting you April when I rang your doorbell or you were watering the plants or whatever. My favorite story is I'm in the 711 after the election. I've been elected county supervisor and I'm 29 years old. Leave walks up to me. She's a supervisor Davis. She's in football night. I'm saying, what's she talking about? She says, my little dog football died. She said, but you know what? She said, you sent me a note, and I got it the week before the election, and you said, give football a pat for me. And I thought, anybody that thinks like dogs, that's the kind of person we want. We'd never vote Republican, but we thought, that's the kind of person we want. Now it sounds corny, but she had a problem about a year later, and she didn't know what to do. She called my office, and we solved it for her. And she was there for life for me, but she also had some faith in the system. So it sounds corny, but I tried to connect with everybody on a one-on-one basis, whether it was where you went to church, what about your kids, where you go to school, what teams do you root for, what's your puppy's name, all those kind of issues. Instead of talking about, I'm pro-life where you or I'm going to cut your taxes, if it got into that, I talk about it. But that connection, you can't stop that connection. And it's a lot of work. I'm wearing a lot of doorbells. I wrote a lot of notes. But it worked.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
