  {
    "episode_id": 91625,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91625.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "Well, I got out of leadership as fast as I could after the campaign committee because the party was moving right and my district was moving left. You asked about Northern Virginia. I talked about the issues, the social issues have changed, but also Northern Virginia has a lot of federal workers. It's pretty secular and it's very multi-ethnic and these are groups that have not been part of the party base. So I had to invent my own base on that. If I went with just where the Republicans were, I'm a 40 percent candidate on a good day. So, you have to understand, you know, where your district is, being in the leadership identified, being with leadership decision, being a committee chairman, where I had jurisdiction over government contracting, jurisdiction over federal employees, I got them, I got them eye benefits, I got them dental benefits. government contractors were about a lot of the contractors like those kind of things basically endeared me to my constituents in a way I could separate myself from the leadership of the party that was becoming increasingly unpopular.",
