  {
    "episode_id": 91625,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91625.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 45,
    "turn_text": "I ended up being general counsel to a public company, and I'm with a law firm now, Holland, a downtown, which is a 1400 member national firm on the rector at George Mason University, so I'm chairman of the board there and teach there. There are a lot of ways to do public service, but I think we all ought to try to live something bigger than ourselves, bigger than just providing for ourselves living in a bigger house and doing kids. I've tried to do that post-Congress, serving on the airport's board and getting the rail to Dulles Phase I completed Phase II funded before I left that to go to the board in George Mason. And at Mason we've done some really great things if you look at where we're climbing the ranks. The thing I like about Mason is We pride ourselves on who we take, not on who we reject. And so it's a lot of first time, first and only person their generation to go to college. Forty percent of our graduates start at the community college and they transfer. We found out we had a survey about 17% of the kids who started the community college and one to go four years ended up completing it so we worked to deal with the community college. Now where you enroll there and you want to go to Mason, we'll give you a Mason card. Let's go to our athletic activities first day and we put an advisor on you so the courses you take you know are transferable. So we're trying to close that and give these people opportunities. Many of them, I said, are first-generation smart people, but that's the only opportunity they're going to have. So I said, you try to live this life outside. And I teach there, as I said, I had 90 kids. I think last semester, I only had 25 of this. You know, I teach for free, obviously. I give them money. They don't pay me anything for it. But the satisfaction of having kids come up after a class eight years, this really challenged me. I really like this. It's good. The hard part is once in a while, you've got to flunk somebody. But they got to try. They got to try. But you owe it to the alumni to make them earn their degree.",
